  "metadata": {
    "title": "Microsoft launches MAI AI models to reduce OpenAI reliance",
    "byline": null,
    "published_at": null,
    "site_name": "finance.yahoo.com",
    "language": null
  },
  "content": {
    "text": "Microsoft unveiled a family of seven in-house AI models on Tuesday at its Build developer conference in San Francisco, a move the company framed as a bid for long-term self-sufficiency and reduced dependence on outside model providers including OpenAI.\nThe flagship model, MAI-Thinking-1, is a reasoning model with 35 billion active parameters and a 256,000-token context window. The company said it was trained from scratch with no distillation from other AI companies' models — a detail meant to appeal to enterprise customers concerned about data lineage. In blind testing conducted by independent raters, MAI-Thinking-1 was preferred over Anthropic's Claude Sonnet 4.6, and it matched Claude Opus 4.6 on the SWE Bench Pro coding benchmark, the company said. The model is available in private preview on Microsoft Foundry, the company's platform for integrating AI models into applications.\nThere are economic incentives behind the push. Hosting models on Azure rather than licensing them from outside vendors means Microsoft sidesteps royalty payments to partners like OpenAI — savings that can be passed down the chain to developers, according to CNBC.\nBenchmarked against McKinsey's requirements, Microsoft's models beat OpenAI's GPT 5-5 while delivering tenfold cost savings, Microsoft AI CEO Mustafa Suleiman said, according to CNBC. \"This is all about long term self-sufficiency for Microsoft and our partners. It's about models you can trust,\" Suleiman wrote, according to GeekWire.\nThe six other models in the MAI family cover a range of tasks. MAI-Code-1-Flash is a 5-billion-parameter coding model now rolling out in Visual Studio Code and GitHub Copilot. MAI-Image-2.5 and a flash variant handle both text-to-image and image-to-image generation and are already live in PowerPoint, with a rollout underway in OneDrive. MAI Transcribe 1.5 supports 43 languages, while MAI-Voice-2 and its flash variant add more than 15 new languages and new voice options.\nThe announcement comes as Microsoft's relationships with its AI partners grow more complicated. Microsoft has revised its partnership terms with OpenAI, capping revenue-sharing payments and ending its exclusive right to sell OpenAI's models. OpenAI has received $13 billion from Microsoft across successive funding rounds, while Anthropic has drawn a separate commitment of up to $5 billion — though that startup counts Google and Amazon, both Microsoft competitors, among its backers as well, according to GeekWire.\nAddressing the Build audience directly, Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella described the unveiling as a turning point in how companies engage with AI. \"We believe the time has come for every company to just move from consuming a frontier model to fully participating at the frontier in the frontier ecosystem,\" he said, according to CNBC.\nMAI models will also be made available through third-party platforms including Fireworks AI, Baseten, and Open Router, the company said.",
    "text_blocks": [
      "Microsoft unveiled a family of seven in-house AI models on Tuesday at its Build developer conference in San Francisco, a move the company framed as a bid for long-term self-sufficiency and reduced dependence on outside model providers including OpenAI.",
      "The flagship model, MAI-Thinking-1, is a reasoning model with 35 billion active parameters and a 256,000-token context window. The company said it was trained from scratch with no distillation from other AI companies' models — a detail meant to appeal to enterprise customers concerned about data lineage. In blind testing conducted by independent raters, MAI-Thinking-1 was preferred over Anthropic's Claude Sonnet 4.6, and it matched Claude Opus 4.6 on the SWE Bench Pro coding benchmark, the company said. The model is available in private preview on Microsoft Foundry, the company's platform for integrating AI models into applications.",
      "There are economic incentives behind the push. Hosting models on Azure rather than licensing them from outside vendors means Microsoft sidesteps royalty payments to partners like OpenAI — savings that can be passed down the chain to developers, according to CNBC.",
      "Benchmarked against McKinsey's requirements, Microsoft's models beat OpenAI's GPT 5-5 while delivering tenfold cost savings, Microsoft AI CEO Mustafa Suleiman said, according to CNBC. \"This is all about long term self-sufficiency for Microsoft and our partners. It's about models you can trust,\" Suleiman wrote, according to GeekWire.",
      "The six other models in the MAI family cover a range of tasks. MAI-Code-1-Flash is a 5-billion-parameter coding model now rolling out in Visual Studio Code and GitHub Copilot. MAI-Image-2.5 and a flash variant handle both text-to-image and image-to-image generation and are already live in PowerPoint, with a rollout underway in OneDrive. MAI Transcribe 1.5 supports 43 languages, while MAI-Voice-2 and its flash variant add more than 15 new languages and new voice options.",
      "The announcement comes as Microsoft's relationships with its AI partners grow more complicated. Microsoft has revised its partnership terms with OpenAI, capping revenue-sharing payments and ending its exclusive right to sell OpenAI's models. OpenAI has received $13 billion from Microsoft across successive funding rounds, while Anthropic has drawn a separate commitment of up to $5 billion — though that startup counts Google and Amazon, both Microsoft competitors, among its backers as well, according to GeekWire.",
      "Addressing the Build audience directly, Microsoft CEO Satya Nadella described the unveiling as a turning point in how companies engage with AI. \"We believe the time has come for every company to just move from consuming a frontier model to fully participating at the frontier in the frontier ecosystem,\" he said, according to CNBC.",
      "MAI models will also be made available through third-party platforms including Fireworks AI, Baseten, and Open Router, the company said."
